version 2.0.3 - "fresh air"
- The Docker app image is rebuilt on the current R release (4.6.1) with
  updated system libraries; three package pins were refreshed to versions
  compatible with it. The published app image can now be pulled directly
  instead of built for an hour. Nothing changes for users of the app or
  the data.

version 2.0.2 - "engine room"
- Under-the-hood refresh of the deployment setup and developer docs;
  nothing changes for users of the app or the data.

version 2.0.1 - "gold standard"
- External validation study against the Human Mortality Database
  (docs/study_hmd_validation.md, scripted in data-raw/validate_hmd_ex.R):
  lemur life tables track HMD to within ~0.4 yr at e0 and e65.
- Round-consistency study condensed for readability; conclusions unchanged.

version 2.0.0 - "2023 refresh"
- Data updated to the Global Burden of Disease 2023 (GBD 2023) round; the
  calendar-year-2023 data is now included (deaths and life tables span
  1990-2023). Ages run 0-95 with the 95+ interval open.

version 1.8.0
- Vignette renamed to lemur-intro (a unique name that cannot clash with
  other packages) and published as a web page on GitHub Pages
  (https://mpascariu.github.io/lemur/).
- README is now a landing page -- pitch, installation, and links to the web
  documentation; the full worked analysis moved to the vignette.

version 1.7.0
- Figure 4 (plotly_decompose, by = "both" and by = "age"): the y-axis now
  spans from the most-negative to the most-positive per-age sum of the
  contributions, using for each side the age that brings the largest outcome,
  so every stacked bar sits exactly between the two axis bounds.
- Tests: the figure-4 regression suite and its comments now match the
  committed epidemiology_palette() colour order (cold-to-warm: the respiratory
  group on top, the maternal/neonatal group at the bottom).
- New HTML vignette (vignettes/lemur-intro.Rmd): a ~10 min tour of the
  package and the Shiny app -- what-if scenarios, cause-of-death profiles,
  life tables and exact age-by-cause decompositions -- with interactive
  plotly charts and summary tables computed live from the bundled data.
- The vignette is published as a web page on GitHub Pages
  (https://mpascariu.github.io/lemur/); README links to it.

version 1.6.0
- Figure 3 (plot_cod): mode_cntr and mode_sex subplots now show dynamic panel
  labels (country or sex) above each panel.
- Colour consistency: causes of death now share the same colour across figures
  3 and 4 via a new epidemiology_palette().
- Figure 4 (plotly_decompose): now fully native plot_ly()/add_bars() for all
  by = cases, reproducing the v1.0.6 stacking geometry (~22x faster); ggplot2
  leaves Imports and plot_theme() is deleted.
- Bug fix (figure 4, by = "cod"): y-axis labels, ticks and gridlines now span
  the full plot height.

version 1.5.0
- Dependencies: drop seven imports (golem, MortalityLaws, purrr, htmltools,
  glue, tidyr, tibble); run_app() no longer uses golem; life tables use the
  inlined life_table_from_qx(); data filters return plain data.frames.
- API: the exported plot functions now return plotly widgets directly.
- Bug fix (figure 4): rebuilt on position_stack(reverse = FALSE) with a
  drop = FALSE palette, restoring v1.0.6 stacking order and colours; a new
  regression suite pins the geometry.
- Tests/dev: graphics suite asserts plotly widgets; CI pins R CMD check env
  vars; dead dev/ scripts removed.

version 1.4.1
- Bug fix: lemur::run_app() (or any UI/server function) failed in a fresh R
  session with "object 'data_app_input' not found" unless library(lemur) was
  called first. data_app_input is lazy data and R only binds data/ objects to
  the package namespace on attach, not on namespace load via pkg::fun(). The
  new .onLoad() binds it at load time so the app starts either way.

version 1.4.0
- Tests: introduce the first unit test suite (testthat edition 3) with ~180
  assertions across data processing, graphics, Shiny UI and server; it runs in
  a few seconds and passes R CMD check with 0 errors and 0 warnings.
- Bug fix: modify_cod_table() no longer drops the 6th row when handed a
  data.table - the deaths column is now dropped by name.
- Bug fix: decompose_by_age() and matrix_to_long_table() preserve the
  region/period/sex labels instead of writing factor integer codes.

version 1.3.1
- The three public datasets data_gbd2021_cod, data_gbd2021_lt and
  data_gbd2021_sdg are no longer shipped as lazy-loadable objects in data/.
  They are now exposed as the exported accessor functions data_gbd2021_cod(),
  data_gbd2021_lt() and data_gbd2021_sdg(), which read the pre-factorized
  data.tables from inst/extdata/. This removes the ~44 MB duplication and
  cuts the source tarball roughly in half.

version 1.3.0
- Performance (startup): replace the bzip2 .rda datasets with lean,
  pre-factorized data.table copies stored as gzip .rds files in inst/extdata/
  (~18x faster deserialization; the app no longer re-converts to data.table).
- Performance (startup): the SDG table (3.1M rows) is loaded lazily, only when
  an SDG mode is active.
- UX: a loading overlay covers the dashboard while data loads.
- Dev: dev/launch_fast.R launches the app via library(lemur), cutting dev
  startup from ~46s to ~8s after R CMD INSTALL.

version 1.2.0
- Performance: replace the ggplot + ggplotly() chain for figures 2-4 with
  native plot_ly() construction (~1.9s -> ~0.10s per interaction).
- Dashboard layout: the four chart panels now fill the browser window and
  resize with it, staying responsive across screen resolutions.
- Figure 3: cause-of-death labels are set a small visible gap off the y-axis
  line in the single and faceted views.

version 1.1.0
- Performance: single debounce point - data_fig() bundles the prepared figure
  data, reduction matrix and every input into one consistent UI update.
- Performance: pre-convert datasets to data.table once at startup (~9x faster
  filtering); re-enable bindCache(); suspend rendering in hidden tabs/panels.
- Performance: figure 4 and the decomposition table show a placeholder when no
  mortality change is applied.
- Bug fix: charts no longer flicker twice; switching modes no longer crashes
  with "non-conformable arrays".
- New knob: shinyOptions(lemur.debounce = <milliseconds>, default 250).

